Date: Wed, 22 Dec 1993 15:14:09 -0800
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: The Enforcement of "Appropriate Caring"

!
--> (flamebait on)

If it's a free society, allow people the freedom to starve as a reward for
laziness or poor social manners who can't find helpful friends or have the
discipline to stay on their prozac, or attend their church programs. Don't
allow 911 emergency service in areas that have had 2 or more gunshots in
the past 3 months; refuse emergency room treatment to those from Sector R.
You'd see some very quick "community policing." Government involvement and
community involvement are nearly mutually exclusive.

A fine line is often blurred, the result of which implies that "caring"
only counts (or is best enforced) through the State and its agents. I for
one am flatly against welfare in its various forms: AFDC (bureaucrat
welfare), the war on drugs (police and corrections welfare), medicaid
(healthcare welfare), state universities (academic welfare), ... . Does
this make me a cold-hearted SOB? Liberals and conservatives alike think so
because my stance doesn't support the government stealing my money to
support a pet morality. "Those poor/evil people/maggots need a government's
intervention to protect us/them from them/us." **** 'em all.

In short, being against governments' views of how to best deal with the
social situations (or "social problems"? careful of the slippery slope...)
does not imply one in not humane. Quite on the other hand, those who
support continued extensions of governmental methods that have
overwhelmingly failed and lead millions in senseless poverty appear are the
foolish saps. From this perspective, it is the liberal/conservative who is
truly cruel. "Teach a person to fish ... " and all that.

It makes one wonder why this knee-jerk reaction to support welfare
recipients is such a common reaction. Oh! The state schools led us to think
that way. OK, if the state says so, it must be OK. Lordy, save me from
thinking for myself! That must be it!

If you were really a preverse politician, say from the south with a strong
racist streak, could you think of a better plan to ruin many people's lives
than building concrete silos for their living places and paying them money
to hang out and watch TV? My oh my, that's a great society!

Government in all its infinite wisdom: shackle entrepreneurs and make it
nearly impossible to build a business, don't allow entrepreneurs to hire
who they want, or pay what they want, (horrors, wouldn't want jobs created
now, would we) and then when a community's employment base collapses and
the good turns to bad, use the IRS and (here in CA) state Franchise Board
to strip us of our wealth to put more police on the street. ("Ask that cop
knocking at your back door.") Let's take our time to feel sorry for people
instead of giving them opportunity. Survival trains hard (Peter Gabriel),
but it does train well. But, jobs for cops is not the same as jobs for the
public, unless the government's objective is more and more cops. Blue
welfare: Our nation's finest -- to protect and serve. Do you feel more
secure? Are you being served? With warrants, perhaps?

This comes back to individual responsibility. Humans have significant
survival capabilities. But as long as the state is the intervener to
address these issues it removes the burden of caring for our fellow people
from our shoulders and moves it to the IRS or its equivalent. That way,
liberals/conservatives can use their support of the government to make
themselves "feel good" and "contributing" because important programs are
being conducted. It's all so clean and doesn't cost time to address the
reality in the neighborhood. 

Which comes back to the earlier point. Give people the clear opportunity to
starve in this country -- by taking away their government-provided
starvation wages -- and stand back as inventive opportunity bursts forth.
I'll bet on individuals instead of the government anyday.

On Beaumont Tower -- the landmark at Michigan State -- is an apt biblical
quote for a university that began as a land-grant college. It reads: "what
so ever a man soweth." If the implications are cloudy, look it up in the
book of Galatians.

I give significant sums to food banks, select church programs, and even my
alma mater (a private college). But this is done because I choose to, not
because of the government or the righteousness of a liberal/conservative
cause deems it necessary. I gladly give to what I consider to be worthy
causes, but as an individual, I want to make that determination for myself.
I hold the government -- and its vocal agents (including the bleeding
hearts) -- as liable for depriving many other individuals of have the
opportunity to make these same choices.

Happy holidays.


