Date: Wed, 22 Dec 93 15:38:50 PST
From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Subject: Vinge's Technological Singularity

Derek Zahn writes:

 I personally think that AI is proceeding right on "schedule", maybe
 even better than I for one would think.  I am very optimistic about
 the prospects for AI, and I am one who "blames" the apparent glacial
 progress on the lack of widely available computer power. ... 

 roughly, progress in AI software (information processing,
 adaptability, behavioral complexity) is "on track" if it produces
 systems with capabilities comparable to biological organisms with
 similar processing power as the machines on which the (real-time) AI
 systems are developed.  ...

 AI software is about on track if it offers the information processing
 abilities, adaptability, and behavioral complexity of a housefly
 when we reach the next order of magnitude of increase in widely-available
 processing power.  ...

 Uploading is a completely different issue, and I have to admit that
 although I'm optimistic about the long-term prospects, I think
 we're way behind here; in terms of technology, we should have
 working computer models of that nematode, but we don't.  

--------

Consider this analogy.  We have some a million lines of hairy
spagetti-code software, written in some undocumented roll-your-own
assembly language, and we want software that does the same thing, but
written in standard C.  That is, we want a "port".

You say you've taken a 100 line peice of software like this, and by
watching what it does, you've written a work-alike without ever
learning this assembly language.  And it only took you a day.  So you
think that ten people should be able to do the big port in three years.

I say I`m going to play with this assembly language, writing things in
it and executing them, until I figure out the language, and then write a
translater from this language to C.  I claim I can get to a work-alike
faster than you, though I admit mine is likely to run slower.  You
counter by pointing out that I have never even ported a 100 line
program from this assembly language to C.  

What does this say about our relative chances?  Very little I'd claim.
You'd expect that you won't be able to translate even small programs
until a translator is pretty good.  From then it might not be much
more work to get it to translate very large programs.  There are
threshold effects in translation.  Similarly, I expect uploading of
larger brains to progress quickly once smaller brains are mastered.  

Robin Hanson



