Right -- and note that I'm not picking on uploading for being behind;
I'm hoping rather strongly that uploading starts to catch up to
the technology that even today could support concrete demonstrations
of theories if they existed.  To me it seems likely that AI will
ultimately help in this task, rather than be preceeded by its
completion -- another advantage that AI has is that it isn't trying
to develop a work-alike in the strong sense, just something (anything)
that shares certain characteristics with us.  And we might not even
agree what those characteristics are!

Much more than AI, uploading science is in desperate need of theorists.
Any takers?  I'll pose just one question to try to provoke some
speculation:

* By what criteria do we judge the quality of our neural simulation?
  We couldn't _exactly_ mimic behavior even if we could keep track
  the I/O behavior required to verify that.  So what do we use then?

derek


