I for one do celebrate the christmas season, and enjoy it significantly.
It's not a feature of religion as much as it is one of fellowship and a
cycle of "keeping the channel open" with many people from my past, and
reaffirming close friendships in the present.

I don't care about the commercialism -- if there's too much on the radio, I
listen to CDs or tapes. If it's too much on TV, I watch tapes or (heaven
forbid) read. And it all leads to REAL GOOD sales next week as retailers
try to reduce their inventory before year end.

And yes, I enjoy the exchange of gifts, especially with some friends with
whom I've been sharing this ritual for ten or twenty years. Often the gifts
received might be a bottle of favorite champagne, or a favorite CD, or a
book of humor I typically wouldn't buy for myself. Gifts given are
similarly something that I suspect the recipient would normally purchase.
It's fun and it strengthens relationships as the years go on. And has
become emergent irrespective if the individuals were bred in christian,
jewish, moslem or buddhist homes. Fact is, we really like each other, and
this is a ritualistic time for its expression.

And I often attend some sort of religious services around christmas,
although I make my decision according to a particular church's presentation
of liturgical music (I love listening to chorale and some choirs). There's
a lot to pick from and, all in all, really good musical shows can be found
-- for free! So what if I gotta listen to some guy talk for a while? I'm
practiced at daydreaming. It's just that the future captures my imagination
much more than the past.

Merry Christmas, Extropians. 

Jingle bell, jingle bell, jingle bell, ROCK!



