Indeed -- it helps prevent them from murdering us.  Who has more firepower,
the inner city or Tim May?  Who is more motivated to initiate deadly force,
a welfare addict or a libertarian?  Of course, in this "inner sanctum" not 
many bums see it -- but we don't have to go very far outside to
encounter legions of silent Detweilers.  However inneficient and morally
objectionable it is, democracy is a Schelling point history has achieved
where we lob virtual bullets at each other via the voting
booth instead of in real life, and use cops to print them out.
What is the point of lobbying against gun control, crypto restrictions, 
and the like if we make no effort bring a politically viable number 
of those virtual bullets into our camp?  It doesn't make sense to 
politically agitate on the one hand, and be against memetic expansion 
on the other hand.  The objections of a little fringe group makes zippo 
difference in D.C.   Flaming the poor won't change the
fact that socialist democracy dominates our contemporary world, and
our technological capabilities to fight back against the tyranny of the 
majority remain quite limited.  When society can shoot back with
_force majeure_, good manners towards society are required, even if 
that means being excrutiatingly politically correct.  Don't dis
the taxman.  Don't get the taxman's stooges all in a lather.

On a private list I hope we can be looser, and in that sense I welcome
the frank comments of Tim May and his critics.  These comments
are indeed valuable for helping us "find our own kind" on the net.
But frankly, I think far too much bandwidth is being wasted on politics.
In "finding my own" I look for mutual needs and understanding in
practical daily concerns foremost.  Politics and future visions
are abstract and largely irrelevent to daily concerns, and should
be secondary considerations.  I'm not against having and sharing these
ideals, of course, but ideals are useless without practical
day-to-day progress.

