Date: 22 Dec 1993 19:49:29 -0800
From: "Martin Minow" <martin_minow@powertalk.apple.com>
Subject: POLI/LEGAL: discrimination laws

Han Y. Huang (hhuang@mit.edu) writes about rights expansion, particularly
sexual harassment and employment discrimination:

> But who decides what is ``sufficiently severe or pervasive''?

Preferably a responsible individual within the company, such as a
senior H.R. (personnel) person. Large companies have an office staffed
to handle these complaints, and written policies regarding them.

If the company proves unresponsive, the matter may end up in a state
commission or, ultimately, before a jury. It is, in fact, the intent
of our legal system that a small number of responsible and disinterested
individuals decide the facts in dispute.

Han continues:

>"In 1991, Congress gave sexual harassment victims the right to a jury
trial and the right to sue for back pay compensation.  The problem is
that while juries are understandably hesitant about condemning alleged
harassers to jail sentences, juries have much less trouble charging
companies with monetary damages."

It is my understanding that civil trials (which would be generally
appropriate for harassment cases) do not result in jail sentences.
Furthermore, civil trials are decided "on a preponderance of the evidence"
while criminal trials require the higher standard of "beyond a reasonable
doubt."

Han continues:

>And unlike many criminal cases,
where a ``smoking gun'' needs to be found, a company can be guilty by
not taking sufficient steps to curb ``hostile environment.''  Merely
doing nothing may not be good enough in court...

This was established, as I recall, in a case in New Hampshire where an
individual was harassed and complained to management who chose to ignore
the problem. The fact that management was aware of the problem and
explicitly let the harassment continue was key: it was more than just
"doing nothing."

Han continues, discussing abusive work environments:

> But once again, who decides what a ``resonable person would find
hostile or abusive''?

A company would be expected to have policies in place that clarify their
expectations for a work environment that meets state and federal laws.
This is not a difficult task.

Discussing "morbid obesity," Han continues:

> So it seems that the disabilities act can cover even voluntary or
mutable conditions that can somehow still be deemed as ``chronic'' or
``lifelong.''  (Confused?  You should be.)  Alcoholism, for example,
is protected under disability law.  What next?

Losing weight, and keeping it off is harder than you might think.
Furthermore, the issue before the court was extreme obesity, which
is generally not a mutable or voluntary condition. There are a few
jobs that cannot safely be carried out by an extremely obese individual;
excepting those, an obese individual should have the same right and
opportunity to earn a living and, in doing so, to work in an environment
free from harassment, as someone of normal weight. A quotation from
Leviticus might be in order here: "Thou shalt not put a stumbling
block before the blind."

Discussing bank lending practices, Han continues:

>And note the recent fuss with banks being accused of ``racism''
because many blacks find it harder than whites to get loans.  People
are upset about this, and once again, government is called upon to
``do something'' about it.  In the meantime, banks are guilty until
proven innocent.

Since Han is posting from MIT, I would recommend that he obtain a book,
published a year or so ago (sorry, I forgot its name), that details
the Boston bank practice of "red-lining." What this book describes is
a situation in the late 1960's as the black ghettos were expanding.
Instead of making loans available to blacks througout the city, they
became available in only one neighborhood (Dorchester/Roxbury). This
prevented city-wide integration for many years. The fact that the bank
executives were Christian and the neighborhoods that became accessable
to blacks were Jewish may well have been relevant.

In the penultimate paragraph, Han writes:

>But, really, can it possibly be ``liberating'' to enslave one's
emotions to react to every small bit of perceived offense?  

There is no evidence that this is the case in the real world: "small
bits of perceived offence" seldom engender lawsuits.

Finally, Han writes:

>Everything that lies wrong with socialism is that unproductive
behaviors -- such as hypersensitivity to offense and the blaming of
everything on discriminatory oppression -- get rewarded.  And by
robbing from the productive, no less.

Pardon me, but I believe that we were discussing the USA here: there
is very little socialism in this country. If these guidelines are
causing problems, it may well be because of an overabundance, and
consequent under-employment, of lawyers. The devil finding mischief
for idle hands, and all that. American lawyers are hardly socialists.

